>> title: Nikujaga
>> category: Stews & Braises
>> tags: japanese, beef, potato, dashi, simmered, weeknight
>> counters: Japanese Home Cooking
>> aka: 肉じゃが, nikujaga, nikujaga, にくじゃが, meat and potatoes, Japanese beef and potato stew, simmered beef and potatoes
>> pairs-with: dashi, gohan, miso-soup
>> servings: 4
>> time: 45 min
>> slack: forgiving — a hard boil shakes the potatoes apart into the broth, and that is the one move it does not come back from

>> step: Dashi 10 : soy 1 : mirin 1 : sake 1, under a drop lid so the liquid is pushed back over the potatoes.
The ratio does the work: dashi 10 : soy 1 : mirin 1 : sake 1.

>> step: fry until the red has gone
Fry @thin-sliced beef{9%oz}(250 g; rib-eye or chuck, sliced for shabu-shabu) with @onion{1}(in 1/2-inch wedges, root left on so they hold) in @toasted sesame oil{1%Tbs}(15 mL) in a #wide pot{} ~fry{4%min}, until the beef has just lost its red.

>> step: turn in the fat 2 min
Turn @potatoes{1 1/2%lb}(680 g; floury, in large chunks with the sharp corners pared off) and @ito konnyaku{5%oz}(150 g; rinsed and cut into short lengths) through @&(~1)beef and onion{} ~fry{2%min}, until every face is filmed with the oil.

>> step: simmer 15 min under a drop lid
Pour @dashi{2%cups}(475 mL), @soy sauce{3%Tbs}(45 mL), @mirin{3%Tbs}(45 mL), @sake{3%Tbs}(45 mL) and @sugar{1%Tbs} over @&(~1)pot{}, lay a drop lid straight on the surface and ~simmer{15%min} at a lazy bubble, until a skewer goes into a potato without resistance.

>> step: reduce to a glaze, uncovered
Lift the drop lid off @&(~1)nikujaga{} and ~reduce{8%min}, shaking the pot rather than stirring it, until barely a spoonful of liquid is left and it clings.

>> step: scatter over
Scatter @snow peas{12}(blanched 1 min in salted water) over @&(~1)nikujaga{}.
